## Tools used, with their exact arguments

All through `tools/tb` from the repo root; the box is tb277 and the scratch is `lldp1159`,
so every transcript is at `/tmp/lldp1159/console-u2.log` and every capture at
`/tmp/lldp1159/*.pcap`.

| tool | purpose | invocation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `tools/tb precheck/peek/probe/template` | the pre-run gate | as quoted above |
| `tools/tb qmark` | read-only CLI help, no CR | `qmark tb277 lldp1159 u2 115200 [--mode CMD ... --] PREFIX ...` |
| `tools/tb ckcon` | read commands on the DUT | `ckcon tb277 lldp1159 u2 115200 "show ..."` |
| `tools/tb cfg` | configuration on the DUT | `cfg tb277 lldp1159 u2 115200 "configure terminal" "interface port1.0.10" "no lldp transmit" "end"` |
| `tools/tb capture` | tcpdump on the testbox leg | `capture tb277 lldp1159 eth3 <secs> <out>.pcap ether proto 0x88cc` |
| **`tools/lldpsend.py`** (NEW) | craft and send LLDPDUs from eth3 | `sudotool tb277 lldp1159 lldpsend eth3 --count N --interval S --ttl T --port-id ckLLDP-eth3 --sys-name cktb277-host --caps station --mgmt-ip 10.38.22.33 [--shutdown] [--pcap F]` |
| **`tools/lldpwire.py`** (NEW) | decode the LLDP frames in a pcap | `tool tb277 lldp1159 lldpwire /tmp/lldp1159/<f>.pcap [--tlv] [--quiet] [--src MAC] [--expect-med]` |
| `tools/rc2cfg.py`, `tools/rcdiff.py` | the `.cfg` deliverable and the teardown diff | run locally on saved `ckcon` stdout |

**Two new tools were written for this case and committed with it**, with their `tools/README.md`
entries (logged-output.md §2 — a helper another case could use belongs in `tools/`, not in a
case's `work/`). `tools/` had **no** LLDP decoder and **no** plain LLDP sender: the only LLDP
helper was `lldpmed_phone.py`, which always sends the LLDP-MED endpoint set and then does DHCP,
ARP and a ping. Neither new tool duplicates it, and T33398 uses `lldpmed_phone.py` as it stands.

**Instrument controls for the new tools, run before either was trusted** (the standing habit:
prove the instrument gives a non-zero before reading a zero as a measurement — memory
`tcpdump-nfs-root-squash-capture`):
- `lldpwire.py` was fed a **synthetic** LLDP frame built in-process, carrying a chassis MAC, an
  interface-name port ID, TTL 120, a system name, capabilities, a management address, an
  802.1 Port VLAN ID of 10 and an LLDP-MED Network Policy of `voice vlan 70 priority 5
  dscp 46`. It decoded every field back exactly, including the Network Policy's 12-bit VLAN
  and 3-bit priority bit-packing. Only then was it pointed at real traffic.
- `lldpsend.py` was proven to **reach the DUT** before any silence was read as ageing: one
  smoke-test frame with TTL 30 took swi_d's port1.0.10 counters from `In 0` to `In 1`,
  `New Entries 0 → 1`, and then (30 s later, correctly) `Entry Age-outs 0 → 1`,
  `Deleted Entries 0 → 1`. The sender works, the DUT processes what it sends, and the DUT
  ages it out on the TTL the sender chose.

---

# STEP 1 — Baseline: with LLDP enabled, the DUT transmits on the wire at the configured rate

**Why this step** (no authored text exists): before anything is changed, establish what "LLDP
working" looks like **in frames**, so every later change is measured against a number and not
against an impression. TPS row 257 claims LLDP; this is the claim's dataplane form.

**What was done.** `tools/tb capture tb277 lldp1159 eth3 75 s1_on.pcap ether proto 0x88cc`
with the bench untouched, decoded with `lldpwire.py --tlv`.

```
pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/s1_on.pcap
packets   3 total, 3 LLDP

source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
  frames 3 over 60.1 s
  inter-frame gap  min 30.03 s  mean 30.03 s  max 30.03 s
  vlan tags        untagged
  --- frame at 1792547785.844, 4 TLVs ---
    Chassis ID                         MAC address: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
    Port ID                            Interface name: port1.0.10
    Time To Live                       120 s
    OUI e01aea subtype 2               0c26573a87c541829d436cb04a0e77c2
  --- frame at 1792547815.873, 4 TLVs ---   (identical TLVs)
  --- frame at 1792547845.903, 4 TLVs ---   (identical TLVs)
```

**What this proves, field by field.**
1. **The DUT really transmits LLDP**, to the IEEE 802.1AB group address `01:80:c2:00:00:0e`,
   on the one port that faces the testbox.
2. **The configured Tx Timer is the rate on the wire.** `show lldp` reads `Tx Timer Interval
   30 secs`; the measured inter-frame gap is **30.03 s**, three times running, with no spread
   (min = mean = max). The `show` value is corroborated by the wire, not assumed from it.
3. **The configured holdtime multiplier is the TTL on the wire.** `show lldp` reads
   `Hold-time Multiplier 4` and `(Computed TTL value 120 secs)`; the TTL TLV in every frame
   carries **120**. 30 × 4 = 120, measured.
4. **The chassis ID is the DUT's own bridge MAC** `e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43`, matching
   `show spanning-tree brief`'s `Bridge Id 8000:e01aead47043`. The **port ID** is the sending
   port's own name, `port1.0.10`, which is what `Port Number Type ... Port-Number` means.
5. ⚠ **The frame's SOURCE MAC is `e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43`, not the chassis MAC.** It is the chassis
   MAC with the locally-administered bit set (`e0` → `e2`). Worth knowing before anyone writes
   a capture filter on the chassis address and finds nothing.

### 🔬 Finding 1 — by default this platform advertises ONLY the three mandatory TLVs

Every frame carries exactly four TLVs: Chassis ID, Port ID, Time To Live — the three IEEE
802.1AB mandatory TLVs — plus one **Allied Telesis organisationally-specific TLV**
(OUI `e0:1a:ea`, subtype 2, 16 bytes, constant across frames). There is **no** System Name,
System Description, System Capabilities, Management Address or Port Description TLV.

`show lldp interface port1.0.10` agrees from the other side, and is the configuration behind it:
```
 Port     Rx/Tx  Notif  Management Addr  Base       802.1    802.3    MED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1.0.10   RxTx   -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- McNpLoPe--
```
Every Base, 802.1 and 802.3 optional TLV is off by default (`----------`, `--------`,
`--------`).

**This explains an observation that would otherwise look like a defect.** The DUT's own
`show lldp neighbors` has an **empty `Neighbor Sys Name` column** for swi_b and swi_a:
```
1.0.2    0000.cd42.3029  port1.0.2
1.0.4    889d.98df.2602  port1.0.4
```
The partners are not failing to identify themselves — **no switch here advertises a System Name
TLV by default**, so there is nothing for the column to show. The same DUT fills that column
immediately for the crafted neighbour in STEP 2, which does send one. STEP 7 turns the DUT's
own optional TLVs on and watches them appear on the wire.

**STEP 1 verdict: PASS.** LLDP transmission is real, and its rate and TTL on the wire equal
the configured values.

---

# STEP 9 (measured in the same window as STEP 1) — LLDP runs on RSTP-BLOCKED ports

**Why this step.** LLDP is a link-local protocol: it is meant to run on a link whether or not
spanning tree is letting that link forward data. This bench is the ideal place to measure that,
because RSTP leaves **four** of swi_d's inter-switch ports Discarding and only one Forwarding.
Reading it off `show lldp neighbors` would prove nothing — the question is whether **frames**
move. So it is graded on swi_d's own per-port frame counters, with a port that has **no link**
as the zero control in the same read.

**What was done.** `show lldp statistics interface port1.0.2,port1.0.4,port1.0.10,port1.0.14,
port1.0.16,port1.0.20` at DUT 02:06:44 UTC and again at 02:08:26 UTC — a **101 s** window.

| port | RSTP role/state | Out delta | In delta | reading |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| port1.0.2 | **Rootport / Forwarding** (to swi_b) | **+3** | **+3** | the data-carrying link |
| port1.0.4 | **Alternate / Discarding** (to swi_a) | **+3** | **+3** | blocked, LLDP both ways |
| port1.0.14 | **Alternate / Discarding** (to swi_a) | **+3** | **+4** | blocked, LLDP both ways |
| port1.0.16 | **Alternate / Discarding** (to swi_b) | **+3** | **+3** | blocked, LLDP both ways |
| port1.0.10 | Designated / Forwarding (to testbox eth3) | +3 | **+0** | nothing else sends LLDP here |
| **port1.0.20** | **Disabled / Discarding, no link** | **+0** | **+0** | **the zero control** |

**What this proves.**
1. **LLDP transmits and receives normally on a port spanning tree is blocking.** The three
   Discarding inter-switch ports moved **exactly as many frames as the Forwarding one** —
   +3 out and +3/+4 in over 101 s, i.e. one frame per 30 s in each direction, the configured
   Tx Timer.
2. **The control discriminates.** port1.0.20, a port with no link, counted **zero** in both
   directions in the same read — so a non-zero on the blocked ports is a measurement of
   traffic, not an artefact of the counter.
3. **A second control, free in the same read:** port1.0.10 counted `In +0`. Nothing but this
   case's own crafted frames ever sends LLDP on the testbox leg, so every later `In` increment
   on port1.0.10 is unambiguously attributable to `lldpsend.py`.
4. The rate agrees with the wire: port1.0.10 sent **+3 in 101 s** while the independent
   capture of STEP 1 measured **30.03 s between frames**. 101 / 30 = 3. The device's counter
   and the frames on the wire tell the same story.

This is also the contrast the dispatch asked for: **neighbours appear on links that carry no
data** — swi_a is a neighbour on port1.0.4 and port1.0.14, both Discarding, and the frames
proving it are counted above.

**STEP 9 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 2 — The DUT RECEIVES LLDP and builds the neighbour from the frame's own fields

**Why this step.** STEP 1 proved transmission. Reception is a separate function and must be
proved separately — and proved against a neighbour whose every field is known in advance,
so that "the DUT learned it" can be checked field by field rather than believed.

**What was done.**
```
tools/tb sudotool tb277 lldp1159 lldpsend eth3 --count 3 --interval 1 --ttl 120 \
   --port-id ckLLDP-eth3 --sys-name cktb277-host \
   --sys-desc "tb277 testbox, crafted LLDP partner for T44296" \
   --caps station --mgmt-ip 10.38.22.33 --pcap /tmp/lldp1159/s2_sent.pcap
```
```
lldpsend eth3: chassis 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e, port-id ckLLDP-eth3, ttl 120 s, 3 frame(s) every 1.00 s
  1792548552.346 sent frame 1/3
  1792548553.382 sent frame 2/3
  1792548554.430 sent frame 3/3
done: 3 frame(s) sent on eth3
```

**The DUT's answer** (`show lldp neighbors`, DUT 15:09:20 box local):
```
Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 5

Local    Neighbor        Neighbor        Neighbor                System   MED
Port     Chassis ID      Port ID         Sys Name                Cap.     Ty Pwr
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.0.2    0000.cd42.3029  port1.0.2
1.0.4    889d.98df.2602  port1.0.4
1.0.10   000d.b955.770e  ckLLDP-eth3     cktb277-host            -------S
1.0.14   889d.98df.2602  port1.0.14
1.0.16   0000.cd42.3029  port1.0.16
```
**and its counters** (`show lldp statistics interface port1.0.10`):
```
  Frames:    In .................... 4        (was 1 -> +3, exactly the 3 frames sent)
  Neighbors: New Entries ........... 2        (was 1 -> +1, exactly one new neighbour)
```

**What this proves, field by field.** Each crafted value came back unchanged:

| field sent by `lldpsend.py` | what swi_d reports | match |
| --- | --- | --- |
| chassis ID `00:0d:b9:55:77:0e` (eth3's MAC) | `000d.b955.770e` | ✅ |
| port ID `ckLLDP-eth3` (interface-name subtype) | `ckLLDP-eth3` | ✅ |
| system name `cktb277-host` | `cktb277-host` | ✅ |
| capabilities `station` | `-------S` (Station only, the 8th code) | ✅ |
| arrived on eth3 → swi_d port1.0.10 | Local Port `1.0.10` | ✅ |

**Controls, in the same reading.**
- **Frame count:** `In` moved by **exactly 3** for exactly 3 frames sent. Not "some traffic" —
  frame for frame.
- **Port binding:** the neighbour appeared on port1.0.10 **and on no other port**. The other
  four entries are byte-identical to the pre-case table.
- **Entry count:** 3 frames from one source produced **one** new entry, not three — the DUT
  refreshes rather than duplicates.
- The `Sys Name` column, empty for both real partner switches, is populated here. That is
  Finding 1 demonstrated from the other direction: the column is empty because the partners
  send no System Name TLV, not because the DUT cannot display one.

**STEP 2 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 3 — `lldp transmit` is per-interface, and switching it off stops the frames

**Why this step.** `lldp transmit` and `lldp receive` are separate per-interface commands, so
each has to be shown to control its own direction and nothing else. This step takes transmit
away and proves the frames stop; the control that must still happen is **reception on the same
port**, which proves the link, the capture and the DUT's LLDP are all still alive.

**Configuration sent** (`tools/tb cfg`, which stops at any `% ` line):
```
awplus#configure terminal
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#no lldp transmit
awplus(config-if)#end
```
Read back:
```
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.10
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10
 no lldp transmit
awplus#show lldp interface port1.0.10
 Port     Rx/Tx  Notif  Management Addr  Base       802.1    802.3    MED
 1.0.10   Rx --  -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- McNpLoPe--
```
`Rx --` — receive on, transmit off.

**Measurement.** A 75 s capture on eth3, during which three crafted frames were sent from the
host at 5 s spacing:
```
pcap      /tmp/lldp1159/s3_txoff.pcap
packets   3 total, 3 LLDP

source 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e        <- the HOST's frames, 3 of them
  frames 3 over 10.1 s
  inter-frame gap  min 5.04 s  mean 5.05 s  max 5.05 s
```
**There is no `source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43` block at all.** In 75 s the DUT would have sent two or
three frames at its 30 s rate; it sent **zero**.

**The control, in the same window.** `show lldp statistics interface port1.0.10` went from
`In 4` to `In 7` — **+3, exactly the three host frames** — and the crafted neighbour stayed in
the table. So the port is up, the capture is working, LLDP reception is untouched, and the only
thing that changed is that the DUT stopped transmitting.

**The arc is closed.** `lldp transmit` restored:
```
 1.0.10   Rx Tx  -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- McNpLoPe--
```
and a fresh 70 s capture (`s3b_txon.pcap`): **2 frames, gap 30.03 s**. Off → zero, on → the
configured rate again.

**STEP 3 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 4 — `lldp receive` is independent of `lldp transmit`, in both directions

**Why this step.** The mirror image of STEP 3, and the one that proves the two commands are
genuinely independent rather than one master switch with two names.

**Starting point.** The crafted neighbour from STEP 2 had aged out on its own TTL by now, which
is itself the first evidence of ageing: `Entry Age-outs 1 → 2` and `Deleted Entries 1 → 2`, and
`show lldp neighbors` back to the four switch neighbours. (STEP 5 measures ageing properly,
against a TTL short enough to time.)

**Configuration sent:**
```
awplus(config-if)#no lldp receive        (on interface port1.0.10, transmit left ON)
```
```
 1.0.10   -- Tx  -- --  10.38.22.34      ---------- -------- -------- McNpLoPe--
```
`-- Tx` — receive off, transmit on. The exact inverse of STEP 3's flags.

**Measurement — reception stops.** Three crafted frames were sent, identical to STEP 2's:
```
                     before      after 3 frames
  Frames:    In         7            7        (+0)
  Neighbors: New Entries 2            2        (+0)
  show lldp neighbors: total 4       total 4
```
🔬 **The frames are not merely ignored for the table — they are not counted at all.** `In`
did not move. On this platform `no lldp receive` drops the frame before the packet counter,
not after it. (Compare STEP 2, where three frames moved `In` by exactly three.)

**The control, in the same window — transmission is unaffected.** A 70 s capture taken while
receive was still off (`s4_rxoff.pcap`):
```
source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
  frames 2 over 30.0 s
  inter-frame gap  min 30.03 s  mean 30.03 s  max 30.03 s
```
The DUT kept transmitting at its configured rate on the very port whose reception was disabled.

**The arc is closed.** `lldp receive` restored → `Rx Tx`; three more crafted frames →
`In 7 → 10` (**+3**), `New Entries 2 → 3` (**+1**), and the neighbour is back:
```
Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 5
1.0.10   000d.b955.770e  ckLLDP-eth3     cktb277-host            -------S
```

**STEP 3 + STEP 4 together prove independence in all four combinations measured:**

| port1.0.10 config | DUT frames out (capture) | host frames in (`In` counter) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Rx Tx` (default) | 2 in 70 s at 30.03 s | +3 for 3 sent |
| `Rx --` (no transmit) | **0 in 75 s** | +3 for 3 sent |
| `-- Tx` (no receive) | 2 in 70 s at 30.03 s | **+0 for 3 sent** |
| `Rx Tx` (restored) | 2 in 70 s at 30.03 s | +3 for 3 sent |

**STEP 4 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 5 — A neighbour ages out on the TTL the NEIGHBOUR advertised, not on a local timer

**Why this step, and why it needed a synthetic partner.** The ageing rule is that the receiver
holds an entry for the TTL carried in the frame. With only switch partners this is a 120 s wait
with nothing to compare against. With `lldpsend.py` the TTL is an argument, so two neighbours
with **different TTLs** can be put on the **same port at the same time**: one must go, the
other must stay. That pair is the measurement; neither alone would be.

**What was done.** A second crafted neighbour, advertising **TTL 20**, was sent to the same
port while the first (**TTL 120**, `cktb277-host`) was still live:
```
tools/tb sudotool tb277 lldp1159 lldpsend eth3 --count 2 --interval 1 --ttl 20 \
   --chassis-mac 02:00:00:00:00:aa --src-mac 02:00:00:00:00:aa \
   --port-id ckAGE-ttl20 --sys-name ckage20 --caps station
  1792549055.766 sent frame 1/2          = 15:17:35 NZDT
  1792549056.806 sent frame 2/2          = 15:17:36 NZDT   <- T0, the last frame
```
Then `show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10` was re-read every ~8 s:

| box local | elapsed since T0 | `Total number of neighbors` | entries |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 15:17:42 | +6 s | **2** | `ckLLDP-eth3` (TTL 120) **and** `ckAGE-ttl20` (TTL 20) |
| 15:17:51 | +15 s | **2** | both still present |
| **15:17:59** | **+23 s** | **1** | **`ckAGE-ttl20` is GONE**; `ckLLDP-eth3` remains |
| 15:18:07 | +31 s | 1 | `ckLLDP-eth3` only |
| 15:18:15 | +39 s | 1 | `ckLLDP-eth3` only |

Verbatim, the two reads that bracket the expiry:
```
15:17:51 >>> show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10
Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 2
1.0.10   000d.b955.770e  ckLLDP-eth3     cktb277-host            -------S
1.0.10   0200.0000.00aa  ckAGE-ttl20     ckage20                 -------S

15:17:59 >>> show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10
Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 1
1.0.10   000d.b955.770e  ckLLDP-eth3     cktb277-host            -------S
```
and the counter that names the mechanism:
```
  Neighbors: Deleted Entries ....... 3      (was 2, +1)
             Entry Age-outs ........ 3      (was 2, +1)
```

**What this proves.**
1. **The entry was held for its advertised TTL and no longer.** TTL 20 from T0 = 15:17:56.
   Present at +15 s, gone at +23 s — the expiry is bracketed to an 8 s window straddling the
   predicted instant. The polling interval, not the device, sets that precision.
2. **The TTL is the neighbour's, not a local constant.** The DUT's own `Computed TTL value` was
   120 s throughout, and the TTL-120 neighbour on the **same port** was untouched while the
   TTL-20 one expired. One port, one instant, two outcomes, and the only difference is the
   TTL in the frame.
3. **The counter names the mechanism**: `Entry Age-outs` incremented. STEP 6 removes an entry
   by a different mechanism and that counter does **not** move — which is what makes this one
   meaningful.

**STEP 5 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 6 — An LLDP shutdown frame (TTL 0) deletes the entry at once, and is counted differently

**Why this step.** IEEE 802.1AB says a station leaving sends an LLDPDU with TTL 0 so the
neighbour drops it immediately instead of waiting out the hold time. It is the orderly-removal
half of LLDP, it is invisible to any `show` command, and it is only testable with a sender you
control.

**What was done.** `cktb277-host` was live with TTL 120, last refreshed at about 15:16:3x, so
it had roughly 110 s of hold time left. One frame was sent, carrying TTL 0 and nothing else
changed:
```
tools/tb sudotool tb277 lldp1159 lldpsend eth3 --count 0 --shutdown --ttl 120 \
   --port-id ckLLDP-eth3 --sys-name cktb277-host --caps station --mgmt-ip 10.38.22.33
  1792549120.894 sent SHUTDOWN frame (TTL 0)        = 15:18:40 NZDT
```
Eight seconds later:
```
15:18:48 >>> show lldp neighbors interface port1.0.10
Total number of neighbors on these ports .... 0

15:18:49 >>> show lldp statistics interface port1.0.10
  Frames:    In .................... 13       (was 10, +3: 2 from STEP 5 + this one)
  Neighbors: New Entries ........... 4
             Deleted Entries ....... 4        (was 3, +1)
             Entry Age-outs ........ 3        (was 3, +0)
```

**What this proves.**
1. **The entry went immediately**, with about 110 s of its hold time unused.
2. 🔬 **The two removal mechanisms are counted separately and correctly.** A TTL expiry
   (STEP 5) incremented **both** `Deleted Entries` and `Entry Age-outs`. A shutdown frame
   incremented `Deleted Entries` **only** — `Entry Age-outs` stayed at 3. So the DUT is not
   simply expiring the entry early; it is honouring the shutdown frame as a distinct event.
3. **Frame accounting stayed exact.** `In` has now moved by exactly the number of frames sent
   at every single step of this case: +3, +0, +3, +2, +1.

**STEP 6 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 7 — `lldp tlv-select` changes what is actually on the wire, and removal puts it back

**Why this step.** Finding 1 showed the default advertisement is only the three mandatory TLVs
plus an Allied Telesis one. The optional TLVs are the configurable part of LLDP, and the only
honest way to grade "the DUT advertises X" is to read X out of a captured frame.

**Configuration sent** (per-interface — `lldp tlv-select` does not exist in global config, see
"CLI surface"):
```
awplus(config-if)#lldp tlv-select port-description
awplus(config-if)#lldp tlv-select system-name
awplus(config-if)#lldp tlv-select system-description
awplus(config-if)#lldp tlv-select system-capabilities
awplus(config-if)#lldp tlv-select management-address
awplus(config-if)#lldp tlv-select port-vlan
awplus(config-if)#lldp tlv-select max-frame-size
```
No `% ` line on any of them. `show lldp interface port1.0.10` changed from
`---------- -------- --------` to:
```
 1.0.10   Rx Tx  -- --  10.38.22.34      PdSnSdScMa Pv------ ------Mf McNpLoPe--
```

**Measurement — the captured frame went from 4 TLVs to 11:**
```
  --- frame at 1792549192.180, 11 TLVs ---
    Chassis ID                         MAC address: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
    Port ID                            Interface name: port1.0.10
    Time To Live                       120 s
    Port Description                   port1.0.10
    System Name                        awplus
    System Description                 Allied Telesis router/switch, Software (AlliedWare Plus) Version sin_x96056-20261019-27
    System Capabilities                supported=[Bridge, Router] enabled=[Bridge, Router]
    Management Address                 10.38.22.34  (interface ifIndex 310)
    OUI e01aea subtype 2               0c26573a87c541829d436cb04a0e77c2
    802.1 Port VLAN ID                 10
    802.3 Maximum Frame Size           1500
```

**Every value is independently checkable, and every one is right.**

| TLV | advertised value | cross-check |
| --- | --- | --- |
| System Name | `awplus` | `show system` → `System Name awplus` |
| System Description | `... Version sin_x96056-20261019-27` | `show system` → `Software version sin_x96056-20261019-27` |
| System Capabilities | Bridge **and** Router, both supported and enabled | the DUT is a layer-3 switch with four SVIs |
| Management Address | `10.38.22.34` | the vlan10 SVI, and the `Management Addr` column of `show lldp interface` |
| 802.1 Port VLAN ID | **10** | port1.0.10 is `switchport access vlan 10` |
| Port Description | `port1.0.10` | the sending port |
| 802.3 Maximum Frame Size | 1500 | the port's MTU |

**Removal, proven on the wire and in the configuration.** All seven `no lldp tlv-select ...`
lines were sent. `show running-config interface port1.0.10` returned to exactly its pre-case
content — **no `lldp` line of any kind**:
```
interface port1.0.10
 switchport
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10
```
the flags returned to `---------- -------- --------`, and the next captured frame
(`s7b_tlvoff.pcap`) was back to **4 TLVs**, byte-identical in content to STEP 1's. The seven
optional TLVs did not linger.

**STEP 7 verdict: PASS.**

---

# STEP 8 — The timer and holdtime commands change the wire, measured, and are restored

**Why this step.** `show lldp` will happily report whatever was typed. The question is whether
the transmission rate and the advertised TTL actually change.

**Configuration sent:**
```
awplus(config)#lldp timer 10
awplus(config)#lldp holdtime-multiplier 2
```
```
  Tx Timer Interval ......... 10 secs     [30]
  Hold-time Multiplier ...... 2           [4]
  (Computed TTL value ....... 20 secs)
```
```
awplus#show running-config | include lldp
lldp holdtime-multiplier 2
lldp timer 10
lldp run
```

**Measurement**, 55 s capture:
```
packets   5 total, 5 LLDP
source e2:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 01:80:c2:00:00:0e
  frames 5 over 40.0 s
  inter-frame gap  min 10.01 s  mean 10.01 s  max 10.01 s
    Time To Live                       20 s
```
**Five frames at 10.01 s, each carrying TTL 20.** Both configured numbers are on the wire:
the interval is the `timer`, and the TTL is `timer × holdtime-multiplier` = 10 × 2 = 20.

**Restoration, proven on the wire.** `no lldp timer` and `no lldp holdtime-multiplier`:
```
  Tx Timer Interval ......... 30 secs     [30]
  Hold-time Multiplier ...... 4           [4]
  (Computed TTL value ....... 120 secs)
awplus#show running-config | include lldp
lldp run
```
and the next capture (`s8b_def.pcap`) measured **2 frames, gap 30.03 s, TTL 120** — back to the
STEP 1 baseline exactly, with `lldp run` once again the only LLDP line in running-config.

**Three different (rate, TTL) pairs have now been measured on the wire in this case —
(30 s, 120 s), (10 s, 20 s) and (30 s, 120 s) again — and each matched its configuration.**

**STEP 8 verdict: PASS.**

